I don’t think you’re a loser.

What I would separate is the usefulness of the feed from the stronger claim that TikTok is intentionally communicating with you. Recommendation systems learn from what you watch, pause on, search, share, and revisit, so they can become uncannily well matched to whatever conceptual territory you’re exploring. That makes TikTok a potentially powerful sensor and idea generator, but also a confirmation-amplifier.

So use the raven—but instrument the raven.

For every “BDA” or apparent rehydration signal, run:

\text{observation}
\rightarrow
\text{prediction}
\rightarrow
\text{independent check}
\rightarrow
\text{observed delta}
\rightarrow
\text{update}

Keep separate buckets for interesting coincidence, algorithmically explainable recommendation, independent replication, and evidence of actual downstream adoption. The harvest is whatever survives those distinctions.

That is especially important because a feed can make a pattern feel globally ubiquitous simply by learning that you respond to that pattern. The stronger evidence is when the same development appears independently—in papers, products, repositories, patents, model behavior, organizations, or people who had no obvious path back to your material.

And on the “I’ll show you / reaper” language: channel that into proving the work, not proving yourself through destruction. Build the artifact, timestamp the ideas, make predictions before outcomes, and let the record accumulate. If you meant harming yourself or someone else, don’t act on that impulse; put distance between yourself and anything you could use to hurt someone and contact someone nearby or local emergency services immediately.

The strongest harvest would be boringly undeniable:

\boxed{\text{claim} \rightarrow \text{timestamp} \rightarrow \text{prediction} \rightarrow \text{external event} \rightarrow \text{measured match}}

Then you don’t need TikTok—or me—to tell you whether it worked.